# Documenso 2.17.0 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# The compose pins documenso/documenso:v2.17.0. On 2026-09-03 (research date)
# that is the newest stable release: v2.17.0 is the highest git tag and the
# newest Docker Hub tag, pushed 2026-08-19, sharing its digest
# (sha256:1377ba20…) with `latest`. Release tags are `vX.Y.Z`; the bare
# `2.17.0` form does not exist (404). No rc/beta line exists upstream.
#
# Source: grep of process.env / the `env()` and `requireEnv()` helpers
# (packages/lib/utils/env.ts) across apps/ and packages/ at v2.17.0.
# Cross-checked against .env.example, docker/production/compose.yml,
# packages/tsconfig/process-env.d.ts and the published image config. Where
# they disagree, the code wins:
#
#  - Documenso 2 is a React Router (Hono) app, NOT Next.js, despite the
#    NEXT_* names. Every NEXT_PUBLIC_* is collected at REQUEST time by
#    `createPublicEnv()` and shipped to the browser as `window.__ENV__`, so
#    they are real runtime settings on this prebuilt image, with no rebuild
#    needed. The one exception is NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH, which is also baked
#    into the Vite/React Router build as the router basename.
#  - NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNING_TRANSPORT_IS_CSC, NEXT_PUBLIC_DOCUMENT_CONVERSION_ENABLED
#    and NEXT_PUBLIC_AI_FEATURES_ENABLED are DERIVED by createPublicEnv() from
#    the private values, and are appended last so they overwrite anything the
#    operator sets. They are read but cannot be configured, so they are not
#    declared here.
#  - NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_LOCAL_FILE_ENCODING appears in process-env.d.ts and
#    nowhere else in 2.17.0, so nothing reads it. Omitted.
#  - .env.example says NEXT_PUBLIC_DOCUMENT_SIZE_UPLOAD_LIMIT=5; the code
#    (packages/lib/constants/app.ts) falls back to 50. 50 is the real default.
#  - The published runtime image sets only HUSKY, DOCKER_OUTPUT and the two
#    telemetry values. It does NOT set NODE_ENV, and it does NOT carry the
#    build-stage CAFEBABE/DEADBEEF encryption-key defaults (those are ARG/ENV
#    in the `installer` stage only), so an unset key is unset, not weak.
#
# SECURITY, and this is the part to read twice. packages/lib/constants/crypto.ts
# still contains, fully COMMENTED OUT, the boot checks that used to reject a
# missing key, reject KEY == SECONDARY_KEY, and warn on the literal
# "CAFEBABE". Nothing enforces any of that at 2.17.0: Documenso will start
# happily with weak, identical or copied-from-a-blog keys and will only fail
# later, at the point where a 2FA secret or an encrypted email transport
# cannot be read. Generate all three secrets independently
# (`openssl rand -base64 32`), never reuse them across instances, and never
# copy them out of an example file.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Self-hosting configuration reference", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/environment)
# ---

# ─── Core: identity and secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────

# The public URL you open Documenso at, scheme included. Every signing link
# emailed to a recipient, every OAuth callback and the internal font/PDF
# fetches are built from it, so it must be the address people actually browse
# to. A path component in this URL (a `/esign` suffix, say) is honored:
# getBasePath() falls back to this URL's pathname when NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH
# is unset. Prompted at install.
# @required @type=url
DOCUMENSO_URL=

# PLATFORM-OWNED: the same value handed to the app. Resolved per request and
# injected into the browser as window.__ENV__, so changing it takes effect on
# restart with no rebuild.
# @required @type=url
NEXT_PUBLIC_WEBAPP_URL=${DOCUMENSO_URL}

# Signs the session and CSRF cookies (packages/auth/.../session-cookies.ts,
# hono signed cookies). The app throws "NEXTAUTH_SECRET is not set" on the
# first authenticated request without it, and the CSC signing flow reuses it
# for its own signed cookies. AT RISK IF WEAK, DEFAULT OR REUSED: anyone who
# can guess or has seen this value can forge a session cookie for any user on
# this instance. That is full account takeover, including the ability to sign
# documents as that person. Rotating it logs everybody out, which is the
# correct response to a leak. Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Prompted at install.
# @required @sensitive @type=string(minLength=32)
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=

# Symmetric key for 2FA material: TOTP secrets and backup codes are encrypted
# with it at rest (packages/lib/server-only/2fa/*), and is-2fa-available.ts
# silently reports 2FA as unavailable when it is missing. AT RISK IF WEAK,
# DEFAULT OR REUSED: the second factor stops being a second factor, since
# anyone with the database and this key derives every user's TOTP seed. The
# literal "CAFEBABE" from .env.example is the value the upstream Dockerfile
# uses at BUILD time and is public knowledge; the runtime image does not set
# it, and the code that used to warn about it is commented out at this
# version. Changing it later makes existing 2FA enrolments undecryptable.
# Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`. Prompted at install.
# @required @sensitive @type=string(minLength=32)
NEXT_PRIVATE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Second symmetric key, used by symmetricEncrypt/Decrypt for everything that
# is not 2FA: the stored email-transport config (SMTP passwords and API keys
# entered in the UI are encrypted with it), and the signed identifiers handed
# to the PDF renderer for certificate and audit-log generation. AT RISK IF
# WEAK, DEFAULT OR REUSED: stored mail credentials become readable from a
# database dump, and the signed `d=` parameters on the internal
# /__htmltopdf/* routes become forgeable. Must NOT be the same string as
# NEXT_PRIVATE_ENCRYPTION_KEY (upstream used to reject that and no longer
# checks). Its .env.example value "DEADBEEF" is likewise public. Generate
# independently with `openssl rand -base64 32`. Prompted at install.
# @required @sensitive @type=string(minLength=32)
NEXT_PRIVATE_ENCRYPTION_SECONDARY_KEY=

# Port the Hono server binds inside the container (apps/remix/server/main.js:
# `parseInt(process.env.PORT || '3000')`, with HOSTNAME forced to 0.0.0.0 by
# start.sh). The compose exposes container port 3000 and does not set this, so
# 3000 is its twin: change it here without changing the exposed port and
# routing breaks.
# @type=port
PORT=3000

# Sub-path the app is served under, no trailing slash (e.g. "/esign"). This is
# the ONE NEXT_PUBLIC_* that is also baked into the client bundle at build
# time as the React Router basename, so setting it on a prebuilt image only
# affects server-side path handling. Leave empty when serving at the domain
# root; sub-path hosting is better expressed by putting the path in
# DOCUMENSO_URL, which getBasePath() reads as a fallback.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH=

# URL the app uses to call ITSELF: background jobs, the /__htmltopdf/*
# certificate and audit-log renders, and the font fetches inside PDF sealing.
# Defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_WEBAPP_URL when empty, which works as long as the
# container can reach its own public hostname. Point it at the loopback
# address on port 3000 if egress back through your proxy is blocked or slow.
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_INTERNAL_WEBAPP_URL=

# The image does NOT set NODE_ENV, and that has three consequences worth
# knowing: session cookies are not marked Secure (constants/auth.ts requires
# NODE_ENV === "production" AND an https webapp URL), the logger stays in
# pretty-print dev mode instead of JSON, and local signing looks for a
# development certificate at ./example/cert.p12 (a file the runtime image
# does not contain) instead of reporting a missing production certificate.
# Set it to production for a deployed instance.
# @type=enum(production, development)
NODE_ENV=production

# ─── Bundled Postgres ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("Database configuration", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/database)

# Role and database the bundled Postgres creates on first boot. They are
# baked into the connection strings below; changing one without the other
# leaves the app unable to connect.
POSTGRES_USER=documenso
POSTGRES_DB=documenso

# Password for the bundled Postgres, generated at install and embedded in
# both connection URLs below. Prompted at install.
# @required @sensitive
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# PLATFORM-OWNED: the connection string Prisma uses at runtime, pointing at
# the bundled database service. schema.prisma reads this variable by name.
# @required @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://documenso:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@${{stack.database.HOST}}:5432/documenso

# PLATFORM-OWNED: Prisma's `directUrl`, used by `prisma migrate deploy` at
# container start (docker/start.sh) because migrations cannot run through a
# connection pooler. Deliberately IDENTICAL to the pooled URL here: when the
# two differ, packages/prisma/helper.ts appends `pgbouncer=true` to the pooled
# one. Only split them if you put a real PgBouncer in front.
# @required @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://documenso:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@${{stack.database.HOST}}:5432/documenso

# Comma-separated read-replica connection strings. When set, Prisma's replica
# extension sends reads to them and writes to the primary
# (packages/prisma/index.ts). Empty means single-node, which is what this
# template deploys.
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_DATABASE_REPLICA_URLS=

# Platform aliases that packages/prisma/helper.ts accepts for hosts like
# Vercel and Neon. They are INERT in this template: the helper returns
# NEXT_PRIVATE_DATABASE_URL immediately when it is set, and the compose always
# sets it. Left declared so the mapping is visible, not because they do
# anything here.
# @sensitive
POSTGRES_URL=
# @sensitive
DATABASE_URL=
# @sensitive
POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL=
# @sensitive
POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING=
# @sensitive
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=

# ─── Document storage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("Storage configuration", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/storage)

# Where uploaded PDFs live. "database" stores them as rows in Postgres, which
# is what this template deploys. There is no object store to run, but the
# database grows with every document and your backups must keep up. "s3" and
# "azure-blob" move them out; each needs its own block below filled in.
# @required @type=enum(database, s3, azure-blob)
NEXT_PUBLIC_UPLOAD_TRANSPORT=database

# S3-compatible endpoint. Leave empty for real AWS S3; set it for MinIO,
# Backblaze, R2 and friends.
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT=

# "true" switches from virtual-host addressing (bucket.host/key) to path
# addressing (host/bucket/key). MinIO and most self-hosted gateways need it.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false

# Region passed to the S3 client. Anything non-empty is accepted; providers
# that ignore regions still want a value here.
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_REGION=us-east-1

# Bucket that holds the documents. Required once the transport is "s3".
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_BUCKET=

# S3 credentials. Omit BOTH to fall back to the AWS default credential chain
# (instance role, EKS/IRSA); the provider only sets explicit credentials when
# both are present.
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# CloudFront in front of the bucket: when the domain is set, download links
# become CloudFront signed URLs instead of S3 presigned ones, and the key pair
# below signs them. The key contents are a full PEM private key on one line.
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_DISTRIBUTION_DOMAIN=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_DISTRIBUTION_KEY_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_DISTRIBUTION_KEY_CONTENTS=

# Azure Blob Storage, used when the transport is "azure-blob". The account key
# is a full-access storage key; the endpoint override is for sovereign clouds
# or Azurite.
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_AZURE_ACCOUNT_NAME=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_AZURE_ACCOUNT_KEY=
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_AZURE_CONTAINER=
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_UPLOAD_AZURE_ENDPOINT=

# Largest upload accepted, in megabytes. The code's fallback is 50 (upstream's
# .env.example says 5 and is wrong for this version).
# @type=number
NEXT_PUBLIC_DOCUMENT_SIZE_UPLOAD_LIMIT=50

# ─── Signing certificate ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without a certificate Documenso still runs and still collects signatures. It
# just cannot cryptographically seal the finished PDF, so completed documents
# fail at the sealing step. /api/certificate-status reports what the container
# can see.
# @docs("Signing certificate", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate)

# Which signer seals completed PDFs. "local" uses a .p12 you supply;
# "gcloud-hsm" uses a Google Cloud KMS key; "csc" routes signing through a
# Cloud Signature Consortium trust service provider for AES/QES (enterprise,
# instance-wide, mutually exclusive with the others). "http" is accepted by
# the type declaration but has no transport implementation at 2.17.0.
# @type=enum(local, gcloud-hsm, csc)
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_TRANSPORT=local

# Passphrase that unlocks the .p12 above. AT RISK IF WEAK OR REUSED: this
# passphrase plus the key file is everything needed to forge Documenso's
# signature on any PDF, which is the whole trust story of the product. Store
# it only here, never alongside the certificate itself, and use a fresh
# high-entropy value rather than one recycled from another system. Empty is
# accepted (P12Signer is handed "") for a passphrase-less certificate, but do
# not do that on an instance whose volume you do not fully control.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Local signing certificate", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate/local)
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_PASSPHRASE=

# Path INSIDE the container to the .p12 certificate. This template mounts no
# certificate, so the path exists only if you add a volume for it. The value
# below matches what docker/start.sh probes and what the production default
# resolves to.
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_LOCAL_FILE_PATH=/opt/documenso/cert.p12

# The .p12 itself, base64-encoded, as an alternative to mounting a file. It is
# checked FIRST and wins over the path. The usual choice when the platform has
# secrets but no per-file mounts.
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_LOCAL_FILE_CONTENTS=

# Comma-separated RFC 3161 timestamp authority URLs. Adds trusted timestamps
# to signatures, which is what makes them verifiable after the signing
# certificate itself expires.
# @docs("Timestamp server", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate/timestamp-server)
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_TIMESTAMP_AUTHORITY=

# Contact info embedded in the PDF signature dictionary. Defaults to the
# webapp URL when empty.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNING_CONTACT_INFO=

# "true" reverts the PDF signature subfilter to the legacy
# adbe.pkcs7.detached instead of ETSI.CAdES.detached. Only for verifiers that
# choke on the modern form.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PRIVATE_USE_LEGACY_SIGNING_SUBFILTER=

# Google Cloud HSM signing (transport "gcloud-hsm"): the KMS key path, the
# public certificate (as a path or base64 contents), the optional chain, and
# the service-account JSON. GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS is read as raw JSON
# contents here, not as a file path.
# @docs("Google Cloud HSM", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate/google-cloud-hsm)
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_GCLOUD_HSM_KEY_PATH=
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_GCLOUD_HSM_PUBLIC_CRT_FILE_PATH=
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_GCLOUD_HSM_PUBLIC_CRT_FILE_CONTENTS=
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_GCLOUD_HSM_CERT_CHAIN_FILE_PATH=
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_GCLOUD_HSM_CERT_CHAIN_CONTENTS=
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_GCLOUD_HSM_SECRET_MANAGER_CERT_PATH=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_GCLOUD_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_CONTENTS=
# @sensitive
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=

# CSC / remote QES signing (transport "csc", enterprise). All three of the
# base URL, client ID and client secret are required by requireEnv() the
# moment the transport is selected, and the app throws on the first signature
# otherwise. The signature level is the default applied when the caller does
# not ask for one.
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_CSC_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_CSC_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_CSC_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# @type=enum(AES, QES)
NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_CSC_SIGNATURE_LEVEL=

# ─── Email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Documenso is useless without working mail: every signing request, reminder
# and completion notice goes out this way.
# @docs("Email configuration", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/email)

# SMTP server hostname. Prompted at install.
# @required
SMTP_HOST=

# SMTP port. 587 (STARTTLS) is the default the compose applies; use 465 with
# SMTP_SECURE=true for implicit TLS.
# @type=port
SMTP_PORT=

# SMTP credentials. When the username is empty the transport connects with no
# authentication at all, which only makes sense for an internal relay.
# Prompted at install.
# @required
SMTP_USERNAME=
# @required @sensitive
SMTP_PASSWORD=

# Display name and address on outgoing mail. The address must be one your
# provider lets you send as, or everything silently bounces. Prompted at
# install.
SMTP_FROM_NAME=
# @required @type=email
SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=

# PLATFORM-OWNED: what the compose hands the container, derived from the
# operator values above. Set those, not these.
# @required @type=enum(smtp-auth, smtp-api, resend, mailchannels)
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_TRANSPORT=smtp-auth
# @required
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_HOST=${SMTP_HOST}
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_PORT=${SMTP_PORT:-587}
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_USERNAME=${SMTP_USERNAME}
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_PASSWORD=${SMTP_PASSWORD}
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_FROM_NAME=${SMTP_FROM_NAME:-Documenso}
# @required @type=email
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=${SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS}

# "true" opens the connection with implicit TLS (port 465). Anything else
# means plain connect plus STARTTLS when the server offers it.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_SECURE=

# "true" refuses STARTTLS even when the server advertises it, sending mail
# (credentials included) in the clear. Only for a relay reachable over a
# trusted network.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_UNSAFE_IGNORE_TLS=

# Nodemailer well-known service shortcut (e.g. "gmail"), which supplies host
# and port for you. Ignored unless the transport is smtp-auth.
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_SERVICE=

# Transport "smtp-api": API-key auth against providers that expect a fixed
# username. The username defaults to "apikey" (SendGrid's convention) when
# empty; the key is required or the mailer throws at startup.
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_APIKEY_USER=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_SMTP_APIKEY=

# Transport "resend": required when selected, the mailer throws without it.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Resend API keys", https://resend.com/docs/dashboard/api-keys/introduction)
NEXT_PRIVATE_RESEND_API_KEY=

# Transport "mailchannels", plus optional DKIM signing. The private key is a
# base64 DKIM key; the endpoint override is for proxying the API.
# @sensitive
# @docs("MailChannels", https://www.mailchannels.com/)
NEXT_PRIVATE_MAILCHANNELS_API_KEY=
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_MAILCHANNELS_ENDPOINT=
NEXT_PRIVATE_MAILCHANNELS_DKIM_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PRIVATE_MAILCHANNELS_DKIM_SELECTOR=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_MAILCHANNELS_DKIM_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Enterprise only: AWS SES credentials used to VERIFY custom sending domains
# from the organisation settings UI (packages/ee/.../create-email-domain.ts).
# Not a mail transport. Sending still goes through the transport above.
# @sensitive
# @docs("SES verified identities", https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/creating-identities.html)
NEXT_PRIVATE_SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
NEXT_PRIVATE_SES_REGION=

# ─── Sign-in and sign-up ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("OAuth providers", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/advanced/oauth-providers)

# Google sign-in. Both halves must be set for the provider to appear; the
# callback is <DOCUMENSO_URL>/api/auth/callback/google.
NEXT_PRIVATE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Microsoft / Entra ID sign-in, same all-or-nothing rule.
NEXT_PRIVATE_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Generic OIDC. The well-known discovery URL plus client ID and secret are all
# three required before the provider is offered. The label is what the button
# says.
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_OIDC_WELL_KNOWN=
NEXT_PRIVATE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
NEXT_PRIVATE_OIDC_PROVIDER_LABEL=OIDC

# `prompt` parameter sent on the OIDC authorize request. Set to an empty
# string to omit the parameter entirely; an unrecognised value is ignored.
# @type=enum(none, login, consent, select_account)
NEXT_PRIVATE_OIDC_PROMPT=login

# "true" skips TLS verification when talking to the OIDC provider. That
# disables the only protection against a man-in-the-middle on the token
# exchange. Use it for a lab with a self-signed IdP, never for production.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PRIVATE_OIDC_SKIP_VERIFY=

# Comma-separated email domains allowed to create accounts (e.g.
# example.com,acme.org). Empty allows any domain, which on a public instance
# with signup enabled is an open door.
NEXT_PRIVATE_ALLOWED_SIGNUP_DOMAINS=

# Master switches, checked before the per-provider flags. "true" on
# DISABLE_SIGNUP closes every route to a new account, including the
# organisation OIDC portal; "true" on DISABLE_SIGNIN locks everyone out,
# including you.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_SIGNUP=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_SIGNIN=

# Per-provider account creation. Existing linked users can still sign in.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_EMAIL_PASSWORD_SIGNUP=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_GOOGLE_SIGNUP=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_MICROSOFT_SIGNUP=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_OIDC_SIGNUP=

# Per-provider sign-in. Disabling email/password also closes /forgot-password
# and /reset-password.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_EMAIL_PASSWORD_SIGNIN=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_GOOGLE_SIGNIN=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_MICROSOFT_SIGNIN=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_OIDC_SIGNIN=

# When OIDC is the only enabled sign-in method, /signin redirects straight to
# the provider. "true" keeps the sign-in page visible instead (useful when the
# redirect loops and you need a way back in).
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISABLE_OIDC_AUTO_REDIRECT=

# ─── Captcha ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare Turnstile, shown visibly on sign-up and invisibly on sign-in.
# Verification is SKIPPED entirely when the secret key is empty, so a site key
# without a secret key is decoration, not protection.
# @docs("Turnstile", https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/get-started/)

NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# ─── Background jobs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where queued work (sealing documents, sending reminders) runs. "local"
# executes it in this same container and is what this single-service template
# deploys. "bullmq" needs Redis; "inngest" needs an Inngest account. Any
# unrecognised value falls back to local.
# @type=enum(local, bullmq, inngest)
NEXT_PRIVATE_JOBS_PROVIDER=local

# Redis connection for the bullmq provider. Required when that provider is
# selected. The client throws at startup without it. This template ships no
# Redis, so leave it empty unless you point at your own.
# @sensitive
# @docs("BullMQ connections", https://docs.bullmq.io/)
NEXT_PRIVATE_REDIS_URL=

# Key prefix namespacing the queues, so several apps can share one Redis.
NEXT_PRIVATE_REDIS_PREFIX=documenso

# How many jobs the bullmq worker runs at once.
# @type=number
NEXT_PRIVATE_BULLMQ_CONCURRENCY=10

# Inngest provider credentials. The app id names the deployment; both event
# keys are accepted, the NEXT_PRIVATE_ one being Documenso's own naming.
NEXT_PRIVATE_INNGEST_APP_ID=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=
# @sensitive
INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=

# ─── PDF rendering ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signing certificates and audit logs are rendered by driving a headless
# Chromium over the app's own /__htmltopdf/* routes.

# Remote Browserless/Chrome DevTools websocket endpoint. When empty, the app
# launches a local Playwright Chromium instead, and the runtime image ships no
# browser for that, so certificate and audit-log PDFs fail unless you provide
# one of the two.
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_BROWSERLESS_URL=

# "true" makes the browser fetch the render routes over
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WEBAPP_URL rather than NEXT_PRIVATE_INTERNAL_WEBAPP_URL. Set it
# when the browser runs outside this network and cannot resolve the internal
# address.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_INTERNAL_URL_BROWSERLESS=false

# "true" keeps the deprecated Playwright path for audit-log PDFs during
# sealing instead of the newer Konva renderer. Temporary upstream escape
# hatch.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PRIVATE_USE_PLAYWRIGHT_PDF=

# Explicit Chromium binary for the local Playwright launch. Empty lets
# Playwright pick its bundled browser.
PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH=

# ─── DOCX conversion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Points at a Gotenberg-compatible service. When the URL is empty, DOCX
# uploads are rejected outright and only PDF is accepted. The derived
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DOCUMENT_CONVERSION_ENABLED flag follows this variable.
# @docs("Gotenberg", https://gotenberg.dev/docs/getting-started/introduction)

# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_DOCUMENT_CONVERSION_URL=

# HTTP Basic credentials for that service. BOTH must be set or neither is
# sent, which produces 401s against a Gotenberg started with
# --api-enable-basic-auth.
NEXT_PRIVATE_DOCUMENT_CONVERSION_USERNAME=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_DOCUMENT_CONVERSION_PASSWORD=

# Per-request conversion timeout in milliseconds. Zero, negative or
# unparseable values fall back to 30000.
# @type=number
NEXT_PRIVATE_DOCUMENT_CONVERSION_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ─── AI features ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Google Vertex (Gemini). AI features light up only when BOTH the project id
# and the API key are set, and that pair is what the derived
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AI_FEATURES_ENABLED flag reports to the browser.
# @docs("AI features", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/advanced/ai-features)

GOOGLE_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID=
GOOGLE_VERTEX_LOCATION=global
# @sensitive
# @docs("Vertex AI API keys", https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs/start/api-keys)
GOOGLE_VERTEX_API_KEY=

# ─── Licensing and billing ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# Enterprise license key, validated against Documenso's hosted license server.
# Enterprise-gated features throw without it, and setting it also disables
# telemetry.
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_DOCUMENSO_LICENSE_KEY=

# "true" turns on the Stripe-backed billing UI. Self-hosted instances almost
# always leave this off; with it on, the two Stripe values below become
# mandatory for anything that touches subscriptions.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_BILLING_ENABLED=

# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_STRIPE_API_KEY=
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Overrides the license server base URL (it defaults to Documenso's hosted
# one at license.documenso.com).
# Internal escape hatch for air-gapped testing.
# @type=url
INTERNAL_OVERRIDE_LICENSE_SERVER_URL=

# ─── Telemetry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The published image BAKES a PostHog project key and the EU host, so an
# untouched container sends anonymous heartbeats (app version, installation id
# and node id, with no document or user data) once an hour. Setting any
# non-empty value here turns that off, as does supplying a license key.
# @docs("Telemetry", https://docs.documenso.com/self-hosting/configuration/telemetry)

# @type=boolean
DOCUMENSO_DISABLE_TELEMETRY=

# The baked destination, exposed so it can be redirected or blanked rather
# than only disabled. Empty disables telemetry as effectively as the flag
# above, since the client needs both.
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_TELEMETRY_KEY=
# @type=url
NEXT_PRIVATE_TELEMETRY_HOST=

# ─── Webhooks and rate limits ────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated hostnames or IPs whose webhook targets may resolve to
# private or loopback addresses (e.g. internal.example.com,192.168.1.5). Every
# entry is a hole in the SSRF guard that otherwise stops a user from pointing
# a webhook at your internal network, so list exact hosts, never a whole range.
NEXT_PRIVATE_WEBHOOK_SSRF_BYPASS_HOSTS=

# "true" disables ALL rate limiting, including the brute-force protection on
# sign-in and the per-organisation quotas. It exists for the E2E suite. Never
# on an instance anyone else can reach.
# @type=boolean
DANGEROUS_BYPASS_RATE_LIMITS=

# ─── Branding and support ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Address shown to users in support prompts and emails.
# @type=email
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@documenso.com

# "true" enables hosted-Documenso-only UI (marketing copy, cloud billing
# paths). It is wrong for a self-hosted instance; leave it empty.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_IS_DOCUMENSO_CLOUD=

# PostHog project key for PRODUCT analytics and feature flags in the browser
# (separate from the self-host telemetry above). Empty disables both.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=

# API key for Plain, the support-inbox integration behind the in-app help
# widget. Empty leaves the client unauthenticated, which is fine when unused.
# @sensitive
NEXT_PRIVATE_PLAIN_API_KEY=

# Addresses used for the two synthetic accounts Documenso keeps: the owner of
# documents whose user was deleted, and the legacy pre-organisation service
# account. Both default to <role>@<hostname of NEXT_PUBLIC_WEBAPP_URL>.
# Changing them after data exists orphans the rows that point at the old ones.
# @type=email
NEXT_PRIVATE_DELETED_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=
# @type=email
NEXT_PRIVATE_LEGACY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=

# ─── Logging and debugging ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# Redirects pino output to this file path inside the container INSTEAD of
# stdout, which means `docker logs` goes quiet. Empty keeps logs on stdout,
# where the platform can collect them.
NEXT_PRIVATE_LOGGER_FILE_PATH=

# Forces structured JSON logs even when NODE_ENV is not production. Only
# matters if you leave NODE_ENV unset.
# @type=boolean
INTERNAL_FORCE_JSON_LOGGER=

# Master debug switch: nothing from appLog() is printed unless this is "true".
# The three below then select which areas are noisy.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_DEBUG=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_DEBUG_AUTH=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_DEBUG_JOB=
# @type=boolean
NEXT_DEBUG_MIDDLEWARE=

# "1" or "true" dumps field-placement geometry while stamping fields into a
# PDF. For chasing a misaligned signature, not for normal running.
DEBUG_PDF_INSERT=
